\date{2026-09-06}
\title{Flatness of the interval}
\author{ulrikbuchholtz}
\taxon{Module}
\import{stt-macros}

\p{The intended model of triangulated type theory is the topos of cubical
spaces, presheaves on the Dedekind cube category. By Birkhoff duality that
category is the opposite of the category of free finitely generated
distributive lattices, so cubical spaces form the classifying topos of the
\em{flat} distributive lattices — the filtered colimits of free finitely
generated ones — and the interval is the generic flat lattice. This module
writes down what flatness says about #{\II} internally (\ref{ub-0040}),
which is nowhere assumed, and relates it to the other axioms.}

\p{Flatness says that solutions of crisp equations in #{\II} come from crisp
solutions in polynomials (\ref{ub-004D}), so a crisp fact about polynomials
can be evaluated at a crisp vertex and transported to every point along the
order (\ref{ub-004A}). With the two crisp points of #{\II} that gives the
disjunctive and the conjunctive properties (\ref{ub-004B}, \ref{ub-004E}).
In the other direction, flatness follows from SQCP together with the
separation of cubes (\ref{ub-004C}). Neither is a characterisation, and
\ref{ub-0041} explains why none in finitely many coherent sequents can be.}

\agda{
open import Foundations.Universes
open import Algebra.Lattice

module Synthetic.Flatness
  {@♭ 𝓘} (@♭ Δ¹ : Type 𝓘) (@♭ I : Lattice Δ¹)
  (@♭ I-distr : is-distributive I)
  where

open Lattice I renaming (0l to i0; 1l to i1)

open import Axioms.UF
}

\agda-imports{
\agda{
open import Foundations.Prelude
open import Foundations.Fin

open import Modalities.Cohesion.Flat
open import Modalities.Instances.Truncation

open import Algebra.Lattice.Instances.Polynomial
open import Algebra.Lattice.Instances.Free.Multivariate I I-distr
open import Algebra.Lattice.Presentations I I-distr

open import Synthetic.Quasicoherence Δ¹ I I-distr
open import Synthetic.Boundaries Δ¹ I
open import Synthetic.Cubes Δ¹ i0 i1
open import Synthetic.Duality Δ¹ I I-distr
  using (Polynomial-Algebra-is-quasicoherent←SQCP)
open import Synthetic.Disjunctive Δ¹ I
open import Synthetic.DisjunctiveProperty Δ¹ I
open import Synthetic.Categories.DetectingEquivalencesCovariantFamilies Δ¹ I
  using (module ♭Δ-Induction)

open import Modalities.Cohesion.FlatConnectedMaps
open import Modalities.Cohesion.Continuity
open import Modalities.Cohesion.FlatLex

open import Core.Arrows
open import Core.Joins
open import Core.SetQuotients

}
}

\subtree[ub-0040]{
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Flatness of the interval}

\p{An algebra over a Lawvere theory is a filtered colimit of free finitely
generated algebras exactly when every homomorphism into it from a finitely
presented algebra factors through a free finitely generated one — Lazard's
criterion, and it is the only characterisation of flatness available in
general. For a distributive lattice #{M} it is the scheme of geometric
sequents, one for each finite presentation #{\langle x_1, \ldots, x_k \mid R
\rangle},}

##{R(\vec x) \;\vdash_{\vec x}\; \bigvee_{n \in \mathbb{N}}\;
  \bigvee_{\substack{\vec q \in F(n)^k \\ R(\vec q)}}\;
  \exists \vec y.\; \vec x = \vec q(\vec y),}

\p{where #{F(n)} is the free distributive lattice on #{n} generators: a tuple
in #{M} satisfying the relations is the image of a tuple of \em{Boolean}
polynomials satisfying them, evaluated at some point of #{M^n}. The inner
disjunction is finite, since #{F(n)} is, and the outer one is not.}

\p{Read in triangulated type theory the external data become crisp: a crisp
presentation has Boolean parameters, since #{\modflat{\II} \simeq \mathrm{Bool}},
and the factorisation #{h : A \to \II[y_1, \ldots, y_n]} is a crisp
homomorphism. The point #{\vec y} of the cube at which it is evaluated is not
crisp, and #{x} is any point of the spectrum. So the statement is: every point
of the spectrum of a crisp finitely presented algebra lies on a crisp cube
in it.}

\agda{
point-hom
  : (n : ℕ) → (Fin n → Δ¹)
  → LatticeAlgHom (Polynomial-Algebra n) (initial-Algebra I)
point-hom n = is-equiv.bwd (Polynomial-ev-is-equiv (initial-Algebra I) I-distr n)

point-hom-gen
  : (n : ℕ) (y : Fin n → Δ¹) (k : Fin n)
  → point-hom n y .fst .fst (Polynomial-gen k) ＝ y k
point-hom-gen n y
  = happly (is-equiv.ε (Polynomial-ev-is-equiv (initial-Algebra I) I-distr n) y)

module _ (@♭ p : Presentation) where

  factors-through-cube : ⟨♭| ℕ ⟩ → Spec (Presented p) → Type 𝓘
  factors-through-cube (mod♭ n) x
    = Σ[ h ∶ ⟨♭| LatticeAlgHom (Presented p) (Polynomial-Algebra n) ⟩ ]
      Σ[ y ∶ (Fin n → Δ¹) ]
        (x ＝ LatticeAlgHom-∘ (point-hom n y) (ε♭ h))

interval-is-flat : Type 𝓘
interval-is-flat
  = (@♭ p : Presentation) (x : Spec (Presented p))
  → ║ Σ[ n ∶ ⟨♭| ℕ ⟩ ] factors-through-cube p n x ║₋₁
}
}

\subtree[ub-004A]{
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{Polynomials are monotone}
\date{2026-10-06}

\p{Evaluating a polynomial is monotone in the point: if #{y \le y'} in every
coordinate then #{q(y) \le q(y')}. The proof is algebra. The assignment
#{x_k \mapsto y'_k t \lor y_k} extends to a map of algebras
#{h : \II[x_1, \ldots, x_n] \to \II[t]}, and evaluating #{t} at #{0} and at
#{1} turns #{h} into maps of algebras that agree with evaluation at #{y}
and at #{y'} on the generators, hence everywhere. A polynomial
#{a t \lor b} in one variable has #{b \le a} (\ref{stt-009W}), so its value
#{b} at #{0} is below its value #{a} at #{1}, and
#{q(y) = h(q)(0) \le h(q)(1) = q(y')}.}

\agda{
module _ (n : ℕ) where
  private
    I[t] : Algebra I (Δ² I)
    I[t] = Free-Algebra I I-distr

    I[t]-distr : is-distributive-algebra I[t]
    I[t]-distr = Polynomial-is-distributive I I-distr

    at-0 at-1 : LatticeAlgHom I[t] (initial-Algebra I)
    at-0 .fst .fst = Δ².t
    at-0 .fst .snd a = refl
    at-0 .snd = mk-is-lattice-alg-hom λ where
      .is-lattice-hom.pres-∨ → refl
      .is-lattice-hom.pres-∧ → refl
      .is-lattice-hom.pres-0 → refl
      .is-lattice-hom.pres-1 → refl
    at-1 .fst .fst = Δ².s
    at-1 .fst .snd a = refl
    at-1 .snd = mk-is-lattice-alg-hom λ where
      .is-lattice-hom.pres-∨ → refl
      .is-lattice-hom.pres-∧ → refl
      .is-lattice-hom.pres-0 → refl
      .is-lattice-hom.pres-1 → refl

    ev-is-equiv = Polynomial-ev-is-equiv (initial-Algebra I) I-distr n

  point-hom-is-monotone
    : (y y' : Fin n → Δ¹) → ((k : Fin n) → y k ≤ y' k)
    → (q : Polynomials n)
    → point-hom n y .fst .fst q ≤ point-hom n y' .fst .fst q
  point-hom-is-monotone y y' le q
    = tr (_≤ point-hom n y' .fst .fst q) (at (path y at-0 refl))
        (tr (Δ².t (h .fst .fst q) ≤_) (at (path y' at-1 refl))
          (Δ².mono (h .fst .fst q)))
    where
      segment : Fin n → Δ² I
      segment k = (y' k , y k) ⦃ le k ⦄

      h : LatticeAlgHom (Polynomial-Algebra n) I[t]
      h = is-equiv.bwd (Polynomial-ev-is-equiv I[t] I[t]-distr n) segment

      h-gen : Polynomial-ev I[t] I[t]-distr n h ＝ segment
      h-gen = is-equiv.ε (Polynomial-ev-is-equiv I[t] I[t]-distr n) segment

      path
        : (z : Fin n → Δ¹) (e : LatticeAlgHom I[t] (initial-Algebra I))
        → (e .fst .fst ∘ segment ＝ z)
        → LatticeAlgHom-∘ e h ＝ point-hom n z
      path z e p
        = unap-equiv ev-is-equiv
            (ap (e .fst .fst ∘_) h-gen ∙ p ∙ sym (is-equiv.ε ev-is-equiv z))

      at
        : {f g : LatticeAlgHom (Polynomial-Algebra n) (initial-Algebra I)}
        → f ＝ g → f .fst .fst q ＝ g .fst .fst q
      at e = happly (ap (λ f → f .fst .fst) e) q
}
}

\subtree[ub-004D]{
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{Flatness in equational form}
\date{2026-10-06}

\p{Unfolded, flatness is the geometric sequent of \ref{ub-0040}: a solution
#{v} of a crisp system of equations is a specialisation of a crisp solution in
polynomials. There is a crisp #{n}, a crisp map of algebras
#{g : \II[x_1, \ldots, x_k] \to \II[y_1, \ldots, y_n]} that respects the
equations, and a point #{y} of the #{n}-cube with #{v_i = g(x_i)(y)}. A point of
the spectrum of the presented algebra is a solution, by its [recursion
principle](ub-001M), and a map out of it restricts to a map out of the free
algebra that respects the relations.}

\agda{
module _ (@♭ p : Presentation) where
  open Presentation p

  crisp-solution : ⟨♭| ℕ ⟩ → (Fin generators → Δ¹) → Type 𝓘
  crisp-solution (mod♭ n) v
    = Σ[ g ∶ ⟨♭| Σ[ g ∶ LatticeAlgHom (Polynomial-Algebra generators)
                                      (Polynomial-Algebra n) ]
                   respects p g ⟩ ]
      Σ[ y ∶ (Fin n → Δ¹) ]
        ((k : Fin generators)
        → v k
        ＝ point-hom n y .fst .fst (ε♭ g .fst .fst .fst (Polynomial-gen k)))

  solutions-factor
    : interval-is-flat
    → (v : Fin generators → Δ¹) → respects p (point-hom generators v)
    → ║ Σ[ n ∶ ⟨♭| ℕ ⟩ ] crisp-solution n v ║₋₁
  solutions-factor flat v r
    = Trunc.ind (λ _ → trunc!)
        (λ where
          (mod♭ n , mod♭ h , y , e) →
            Trunc.η
              ( mod♭ n
              , mod♭ ( LatticeAlgHom-∘ h (Presented-ι p)
                     , Presented-ι-respects p h)
              , y
              , λ k → sym (point-hom-gen generators v k)
                    ∙ sym (Presented-rec-β p (point-hom generators v) r
                            (Polynomial-gen k))
                    ∙ happly (ap (λ f → f .fst .fst) e)
                        (ι-quot (Polynomial-gen k))))
        (flat p (Presented-rec p (point-hom generators v) r))
}
}

\subtree[ub-004B]{
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Flatness gives the disjunctive property}
\date{2026-10-06}

\p{If the interval is flat and its boundary inclusion is
#{\flat}-surjective, then #{i \lor j = 1} implies #{i = 1} or #{j = 1}. The
pair #{(i, j)} solves #{x_0 \lor x_1 = 1}, so by \ref{ub-004D} it is merely
#{(q_0(y), q_1(y))} for crisp polynomials #{q_0 \lor q_1 = 1} in #{n}
variables and some point #{y}. Evaluating at the bottom vertex, which is
crisp, gives \em{crisp} #{a = q_0(0)} and #{b = q_1(0)} with
#{a \lor b = 1}, and for crisp elements the disjunctive property holds by
\ref{stt-011A}. Say #{a = 1}. Then #{1 = q_0(0) \le q_0(y) = i} by
\ref{ub-004A}.}

\p{This replaces the directedness and the separation by types with initial
objects that \ref{stt-0114} uses by flatness, and needs neither duality nor
SQCP.}

\agda{
private
  module P2 = Lattice (Polynomial-Lattice 2)

  x₀ x₁ : Polynomials 2
  x₀ = Polynomial-gen fzero
  x₁ = Polynomial-gen (fsuc fzero)

  two-point : Δ¹ → Δ¹ → Fin 2 → Δ¹
  two-point i j fzero = i
  two-point i j (fsuc fzero) = j

  point-hom-is-hom
    : (n : ℕ) (y : Fin n → Δ¹)
    → is-lattice-hom (Polynomial-Lattice n) I (point-hom n y .fst .fst)
  point-hom-is-hom n y = un-is-lattice-alg-hom (point-hom n y .snd)

  composite-is-hom
    : (n : ℕ) (y : Fin n → Δ¹)
      (g : LatticeAlgHom (Polynomial-Algebra 2) (Polynomial-Algebra n))
    → is-lattice-hom (Polynomial-Lattice 2) I
        (LatticeAlgHom-∘ (point-hom n y) g .fst .fst)
  composite-is-hom n y g
    = un-is-lattice-alg-hom (LatticeAlgHom-∘ (point-hom n y) g .snd)

  ∨-presentation : Presentation
  ∨-presentation = mk-presentation 2 1 (λ _ → x₀ P2.∨ x₁) (λ _ → P2.1l)

  two-point-∨
    : (i j : Δ¹) → i ∨ j ＝ i1
    → respects ∨-presentation (point-hom 2 (two-point i j))
  two-point-∨ i j e _
    = point-hom-is-hom 2 (two-point i j) .is-lattice-hom.pres-∨ {x₀} {x₁}
    ∙ ap₂ _∨_ (point-hom-gen 2 (two-point i j) fzero)
        (point-hom-gen 2 (two-point i j) (fsuc fzero))
    ∙ e
    ∙ sym (point-hom-is-hom 2 (two-point i j) .is-lattice-hom.pres-1)

  vertex-∨
    : (n : ℕ) (c : Fin n → Δ¹)
      (g : LatticeAlgHom (Polynomial-Algebra 2) (Polynomial-Algebra n))
    → respects ∨-presentation g
    → ∨=1 ( LatticeAlgHom-∘ (point-hom n c) g .fst .fst x₀
          , LatticeAlgHom-∘ (point-hom n c) g .fst .fst x₁)
  vertex-∨ n c g r
    = sym (composite-is-hom n c g .is-lattice-hom.pres-∨ {x₀} {x₁})
    ∙ ap (point-hom n c .fst .fst) (r fzero)
    ∙ composite-is-hom n c g .is-lattice-hom.pres-1

module _
  (@♭ ∂Δ¹-incl-♭-surjective : is-♭-surjective ∂Δ¹-incl)
  (flat : interval-is-flat)
  where

  disjunctive-property←flat
    : (i j : Δ¹) → i ∨ j ＝ i1 → (i ＝ i1) * (j ＝ i1)
  disjunctive-property←flat i j e
    = Trunc.ind (λ _ → is-truncated←is-prop generating-fam-disjunctive-is-prop)
        (λ where (mod♭ n , mod♭ (g , r) , y , s) → from-cube n g r y s)
        (solutions-factor ∨-presentation flat
          (two-point i j) (two-point-∨ i j e))
    where
      from-cube
        : (@♭ n : ℕ)
          (@♭ g : LatticeAlgHom (Polynomial-Algebra 2) (Polynomial-Algebra n))
          (@♭ r : respects ∨-presentation g)
          (y : Fin n → Δ¹)
        → ((k : Fin 2)
          → two-point i j k
          ＝ point-hom n y .fst .fst (g .fst .fst (Polynomial-gen k)))
        → (i ＝ i1) * (j ＝ i1)
      from-cube n g r y s
        = *-prop-rec generating-fam-disjunctive-is-prop
            (λ a → ι₁ (lift fzero a))
            (λ b → ι₂ (lift (fsuc fzero) b))
            (generating-fam-disjunctive←∨=1-crisp ∂Δ¹-incl-♭-surjective
              ( LatticeAlgHom-∘ (point-hom n (λ _ → i0)) g .fst .fst x₀
              , LatticeAlgHom-∘ (point-hom n (λ _ → i0)) g .fst .fst x₁)
              (vertex-∨ n (λ _ → i0) g r))
        where
          lift
            : (k : Fin 2)
            → point-hom n (λ _ → i0) .fst .fst (g .fst .fst (Polynomial-gen k))
              ＝ i1
            → two-point i j k ＝ i1
          lift k a
            = s k
            ∙ ＝1←1≤
                (≤-trans (≤←＝ (sym a))
                  (point-hom-is-monotone n (λ _ → i0) y (λ _ → 0-init)
                    (g .fst .fst (Polynomial-gen k))))
}
}

\subtree[ub-004E]{
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Flatness gives the conjunctive property}
\date{2026-10-06}

\p{Dually, if the interval is flat and its boundary inclusion is
#{\flat}-surjective, then #{i \land j = 0} implies #{i = 0} or #{j = 0}: the
argument of \ref{ub-004B} with the top vertex in place of the bottom one. The
crisp case is again a case distinction on the crisp element #{i}: if
#{i = 0} the left clause holds, and if #{i = 1} then #{j = i \land j = 0}.}

\agda{
private
  ∧-presentation : Presentation
  ∧-presentation = mk-presentation 2 1 (λ _ → x₀ P2.∧ x₁) (λ _ → P2.0l)

  two-point-∧
    : (i j : Δ¹) → i ∧ j ＝ i0
    → respects ∧-presentation (point-hom 2 (two-point i j))
  two-point-∧ i j e _
    = point-hom-is-hom 2 (two-point i j) .is-lattice-hom.pres-∧ {x₀} {x₁}
    ∙ ap₂ _∧_ (point-hom-gen 2 (two-point i j) fzero)
        (point-hom-gen 2 (two-point i j) (fsuc fzero))
    ∙ e
    ∙ sym (point-hom-is-hom 2 (two-point i j) .is-lattice-hom.pres-0)

  vertex-∧
    : (n : ℕ) (c : Fin n → Δ¹)
      (g : LatticeAlgHom (Polynomial-Algebra 2) (Polynomial-Algebra n))
    → respects ∧-presentation g
    → ( LatticeAlgHom-∘ (point-hom n c) g .fst .fst x₀
      ∧ LatticeAlgHom-∘ (point-hom n c) g .fst .fst x₁) ＝ i0
  vertex-∧ n c g r
    = sym (composite-is-hom n c g .is-lattice-hom.pres-∧ {x₀} {x₁})
    ∙ ap (point-hom n c .fst .fst) (r fzero)
    ∙ composite-is-hom n c g .is-lattice-hom.pres-0

  =0-is-prop : {i j : Δ¹} → is-prop ((i ＝ i0) * (j ＝ i0))
  =0-is-prop = *-is-prop (carrier-is-set _ _) (carrier-is-set _ _)

module _
  (@♭ ∂Δ¹-incl-♭-surjective : is-♭-surjective ∂Δ¹-incl)
  where
  open ♭Δ-Induction ∂Δ¹-incl-♭-surjective

  conjunctive-property-crisp
    : (@♭ i j : Δ¹) → i ∧ j ＝ i0 → (i ＝ i0) * (j ＝ i0)
  conjunctive-property-crisp i j
    = ♭Δ¹-ind
        (λ i → (i ∧ j ＝ i0 → (i ＝ i0) * (j ＝ i0))
             , →-is-prop =0-is-prop)
        (λ _ → ι₁ refl)
        (λ h → ι₂ (sym (∧-comm ∙ 1-top) ∙ h))
        i

  module _ (flat : interval-is-flat) where

    conjunctive-property←flat
      : (i j : Δ¹) → i ∧ j ＝ i0 → (i ＝ i0) * (j ＝ i0)
    conjunctive-property←flat i j e
      = Trunc.ind (λ _ → is-truncated←is-prop =0-is-prop)
          (λ where (mod♭ n , mod♭ (g , r) , y , s) → from-cube n g r y s)
          (solutions-factor ∧-presentation flat
            (two-point i j) (two-point-∧ i j e))
      where
        from-cube
          : (@♭ n : ℕ)
            (@♭ g : LatticeAlgHom (Polynomial-Algebra 2) (Polynomial-Algebra n))
            (@♭ r : respects ∧-presentation g)
            (y : Fin n → Δ¹)
          → ((k : Fin 2)
            → two-point i j k
            ＝ point-hom n y .fst .fst (g .fst .fst (Polynomial-gen k)))
          → (i ＝ i0) * (j ＝ i0)
        from-cube n g r y s
          = *-prop-rec =0-is-prop
              (λ a → ι₁ (lift fzero a))
              (λ b → ι₂ (lift (fsuc fzero) b))
              (conjunctive-property-crisp
                (LatticeAlgHom-∘ (point-hom n (λ _ → i1)) g .fst .fst x₀)
                (LatticeAlgHom-∘ (point-hom n (λ _ → i1)) g .fst .fst x₁)
                (vertex-∧ n (λ _ → i1) g r))
          where
            lift
              : (k : Fin 2)
              → point-hom n (λ _ → i1) .fst .fst
                  (g .fst .fst (Polynomial-gen k))
                ＝ i0
              → two-point i j k ＝ i0
            lift k a
              = s k
              ∙ ＝0←≤0
                  (≤-trans
                    (point-hom-is-monotone n y (λ _ → i1) (λ _ → 1-top)
                      (g .fst .fst (Polynomial-gen k)))
                    (≤←＝ a))
}
}

\subtree[ub-004C]{
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Cubes separating implies flatness}
\date{2026-10-06}

\p{If [SQCP](stt-00TC) holds and the [cubes separate](stt-00J7), then the
interval is flat. Fix a crisp presentation with algebra #{A}, and consider
the subtype #{C \subseteq \Spec(A)} of points that merely lie on a crisp cube.
Flatness says that the inclusion #{C \hookrightarrow \Spec(A)} is an
equivalence, so by separation it is enough that every crisp map
#{g : \II^m \to \Spec(A)} from a crisp cube lands in #{C}; the crisp points
are the case #{m = 0}. By the [generalised Phoa principle](ub-001W),
#{\II[y_1, \ldots, y_m]} is quasicoherent, so maps
#{\II^m \cong \Spec(\II[y_1, \ldots, y_m]) \to \Spec(A)} are exactly maps of
algebras #{h : A \to \II[y_1, \ldots, y_m]}, and #{g} is the crisp such #{h}
followed by evaluation. So #{g(y)} lies on the crisp cube #{h} at #{y}.}

\p{In the intended model this is the observation that every element of a
presheaf lies in the image of a representable; separation by cubes is how
type theory sees that. Separation quantifies over all crisp types, so this
is no finite axiom either, in keeping with \ref{ub-0041}.}

\agda{
module _
  (@♭ sqcp : SQCP)
  (@♭ □-separates : family-separates □^_)
  (@♭ p : Presentation)
  where

  private
    A = Presented p

    covered : Spec A → Type 𝓘
    covered x = ║ Σ[ n ∶ ⟨♭| ℕ ⟩ ] factors-through-cube p n x ║₋₁

    Covered : Type 𝓘
    Covered = Σ (Spec A) covered

    Covered-incl-is-embedding : is-embedding (fst {B = covered})
    Covered-incl-is-embedding = Subtype.Σ̃-π-is-embedding
        (mk-subtype {family = covered} (λ _ → ║-║₋₁-is-prop))

    hom≃ : (m : ℕ) → is-equiv (Spec-map {A = A} {Polynomial-Algebra m})
    hom≃ m
      = Spec-map-is-equiv←quasicoherent (Polynomial-Algebra m)
          (Polynomial-Algebra-is-quasicoherent←SQCP sqcp m) A

    vertices : (m : ℕ) → Spec (Polynomial-Algebra m) → (Fin m → Δ¹)
    vertices = Polynomial-ev (initial-Algebra I) I-distr

    vertices-is-equiv : (m : ℕ) → is-equiv (vertices m)
    vertices-is-equiv = Polynomial-ev-is-equiv (initial-Algebra I) I-distr

    cube-hom
      : (m : ℕ) → ((Fin m → Δ¹) → Spec A)
      → LatticeAlgHom A (Polynomial-Algebra m)
    cube-hom m g = is-equiv.bwd (hom≃ m) (g ∘ vertices m)

    cube-hom-at
      : (m : ℕ) (g : (Fin m → Δ¹) → Spec A) (z : Fin m → Δ¹)
      → g z ＝ LatticeAlgHom-∘ (point-hom m z) (cube-hom m g)
    cube-hom-at m g z
      = ap g (sym (is-equiv.ε (vertices-is-equiv m) z))
      ∙ sym (happly (is-equiv.ε (hom≃ m) (g ∘ vertices m)) (point-hom m z))

    covered←cube
      : (@♭ m : ℕ) (@♭ g : (Fin m → Δ¹) → Spec A) (z : Fin m → Δ¹)
      → covered (g z)
    covered←cube m g z
      = Trunc.η (mod♭ m , mod♭ (cube-hom m g) , z , cube-hom-at m g z)

    Covered-incl-is-♭-equiv : is-♭-equiv (fst {B = covered})
    Covered-incl-is-♭-equiv
      = is-equiv←section←injective
          (♭-map-is-injective (unap Covered-incl-is-embedding))
          ( (λ where
              (mod♭ x) → mod♭ (x , covered←cube 0 (λ _ → x) (λ ())))
          , (λ where (mod♭ x) → refl))

    postcomp-Covered-incl-is-♭-equiv
      : (@♭ m : ℕ) → is-♭-equiv (postcomp (□^ m) (fst {B = covered}))
    postcomp-Covered-incl-is-♭-equiv m
      = is-equiv←section←injective
          (♭-map-is-injective
            (unap (postcomp-is-embedding Covered-incl-is-embedding)))
          ( (λ where
              (mod♭ g) → mod♭ λ z →
                ( g z
                , tr covered (ap g (is-equiv.η (cube←□-is-equiv) z))
                    (covered←cube m (g ∘ is-equiv.bwd cube←□-is-equiv)
                      (cube←□ z))))
          , (λ where (mod♭ g) → refl))

    Covered-incl-is-equiv : is-equiv (fst {B = covered})
    Covered-incl-is-equiv
      = □-separates (mk-arrow (fst {B = covered}))
          Covered-incl-is-♭-equiv
          (λ {m} → postcomp-Covered-incl-is-♭-equiv m)

  covered←□-separates : (x : Spec A) → covered x
  covered←□-separates x
    = tr covered (is-equiv.ε Covered-incl-is-equiv x)
        (is-equiv.bwd Covered-incl-is-equiv x .snd)

interval-is-flat←□-separates
  : @♭ SQCP → @♭ family-separates □^_ → interval-is-flat
interval-is-flat←□-separates sqcp sep p = covered←□-separates sqcp sep p
}
}

\subtree[ub-0041]{
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Why flatness is not a finite matter}

\p{Two coherent sequents that every flat lattice satisfies are
\em{nontriviality}, #{0 \neq 1}, and \em{locality} in both forms,
#{x \lor y = 1 \Rightarrow x = 1 \lor y = 1} and
#{x \land y = 0 \Rightarrow x = 0 \lor y = 0}: in a free lattice, evaluation
at the bottom vertex of the cube is a lattice map to #{\mathrm{Bool}} whose
kernel is #{\{1\}}, and evaluation at the top vertex one whose kernel is
#{\{0\}}, and all three sequents are preserved by filtered colimits. Their
orthogonality forms are the [disjunctive](stt-00DY) and
[conjunctive](stt-00TW) conditions, and \citek{SY25} call them (NT), (L) and
(cL). They do not characterise flatness, and no finite list of coherent
sequents does.}

\p{Internally the two localities follow from flatness once the crisp points
of #{\II} are #{0} and #{1} (\ref{ub-004B}, \ref{ub-004E}), by evaluating
at the vertices. Nontriviality does not: flatness only transfers a crisp
#{0 = 1}, evaluated at a crisp vertex, to the given one, and the trivial
lattice is flat. Nontriviality has to be assumed crisply, as it is in
triangulated type theory.}

\p{\strong{They do not suffice.} Finitely presented distributive lattices are
finite, and a finite lattice #{A} is flat exactly when it is a retract of a
free one; under Birkhoff duality that says that the poset #{J(A)} of
join-irreducibles is a poset retract of a cube, which is to say a lattice.
Nontriviality and the two localities, on the other hand, say only that
#{J(A)} is nonempty with a top and a bottom. So the downset lattice of the
bounded poset #{\{0 < a, b < c, d < 1\}} with #{a, b} both below #{c, d} is
nontrivial, local and colocal, and not flat. That poset is the smallest
bounded poset that is not a lattice.}

\p{\strong{No finite list does.} The models of a coherent theory are closed
under ultraproducts, and the flat lattices are not, because the dimension
#{n} in the scheme above cannot be bounded in terms of the presentation. Take
#{A = D(P)} for the poset #{P} just named, so that a homomorphism
#{A \to F(m) = D([1]^m)} is a monotone map #{g : [1]^m \to P}, and let #{g}
send the vertex #{e_i} to #{a} for odd #{i} and to #{b} for even #{i}, a sum
#{e_i + e_j} to #{c} or #{d} according to the parity of #{i + j}, and every
vertex of weight at least three to #{1}. A factorisation of #{g} through a
free lattice #{F(n)} is a factorisation of the poset map through a lattice
#{L} that is a retract of #{[1]^n}, and in #{L} the elements
#{x_i \lor x_{i+1}} for #{i} odd are pairwise distinct: were two of them equal
to a common #{u}, some cross join #{x_i \lor x_k} of the other parity would
lie below #{u}, putting #{u} in both #{r^{-1}(\uparrow c)} and
#{r^{-1}(\uparrow d)} and hence in #{r^{-1}(1)}. So #{|L| \ge m/2} and
#{n \ge \log_2(m/2)}. The maps #{g_m} therefore assemble to a homomorphism
from #{A} into a nonprincipal ultraproduct of the #{F(m)} which factors
through no #{F(n)} at all.}
}
